<main class="wrap">

  <section class="grid">

    <article class="card col-8">

      <h2>Quick Wins (Try These First)</h2>

      <ol>

        <li><strong>Toggle Airplane Mode</strong> for 10–15 seconds to force a fresh tower handshake.</li>

        <li><strong>Restart phone</strong> to flush radio/modem and background network tasks.</li>

        <li><strong>Move closer to the Wi-Fi router</strong> and avoid walls/metal; use 5 GHz when near, 2.4 GHz for distance.</li>

        <li><strong>Turn off VPN</strong> if you don’t need it; some routes can slow peak speeds (exceptions later).</li>

        <li><strong>Close bandwidth-hungry apps</strong> (cloud backup, updates, torrents, auto-play feeds).</li>

      </ol>

      <div class="good"><strong>Result:</strong> Many devices gain 10–40% throughput from just these resets and usage tweaks.</div>

      <h2>Mobile Data Tweaks (4G/5G)</h2>

      <h3>1) Lock the Preferred Network Type Smartly</h3>

      <p>Go to <span class="kbd">Settings → Network & Internet → SIMs → Preferred network type</span>. Choose <strong>5G/4G (Auto)</strong>. If your 5G is flaky, test <strong>4G only</strong> for steadier speeds and lower ping.</p>

      <h3>2) Refresh APN & Clean Out Bad Entries</h3>

      <ol class="steps">

        <li>Open <span class="kbd">Settings → Mobile Network → Access Point Names</span>.</li>

        <li>Tap the default APN → compare with carrier site → fix typos (APN, MMSC, MCC/MNC).</li>

        <li>Delete duplicate/old APNs → keep one clean default → <strong>Save</strong>.</li>

      </ol>

      <p class="note">Carriers sometimes push APN updates; re-select the official APN if speed drops after a system update.</p>

      <h3>3) Force a New Cell & Band</h3>

      <p>Turn <strong>Airplane Mode on → off</strong>. Moving a few meters (or near a window) can switch you to a less congested cell/band with better SNR.</p>

      <h3>4) Use the Right SIM Slot</h3>

      <p>On dual-SIM phones, Slot 1 often has better antenna/radio support. Try swapping the SIM if speeds are poor.</p>

      <h3>5) Turn Off Data Saver & Background Limits (Temporarily)</h3>

      <p>These can throttle syncs or VPNs. Check <span class="kbd">Settings → Network & Internet → Data Saver</span> and per-app battery restrictions.</p>

      <h2>DNS Acceleration (Works for Both Data & Wi-Fi)</h2>

      <p>Fast DNS reduces time-to-first-byte for websites and sometimes improves route selection.</p>

      <ol class="steps">

        <li>Go to <span class="kbd">Settings → Network & Internet → Private DNS</span>.</li>

        <li>Select <strong>Private DNS provider hostname</strong>.</li>

        <li>Try one at a time: <span class="kbd">dns.google</span>, <span class="kbd">one.one.one.one</span>, or <span class="kbd">dns.adguard-dns.com</span>.</li>

      </ol>

      <div class="good">Test each with speedtest + browsing. Keep the one with the snappiest page loads, not just peak Mbps.</div>

      <h2>Browser & App Optimizations</h2>

      <ul>

        <li><strong>Disable heavy extensions</strong> (ad-blockers with strict filters can slow pages; try lighter lists).</li>

        <li><strong>Clear cache</strong> in your browser and streaming apps to fix stale/fragmented data.</li>

        <li><strong>Use Lite modes</strong> where available (Reddit Lite, Facebook Lite, YouTube quality cap on mobile).</li>

        <li><strong>Turn off autoplay</strong> and background video in social apps.</li>

        <li><strong>Schedule backups/updates</strong> for off-peak hours or while on Wi-Fi.</li>

      </ul>

      <h2>Wi-Fi: Fix Congestion & Signal Quality</h2>

      <h3>6) Choose the Best Band & Channel</h3>

      <p>On the router: prefer <strong>5 GHz</strong> for high speeds; keep <strong>2.4 GHz</strong> for range/older devices. Use <strong>channel 36/40/44/48</strong> on 5 GHz; avoid crowded DFS if your area is noisy.</p>

      <h3>7) Adjust Channel Width</h3>

      <p>For apartments, set <strong>40 MHz</strong> width on 5 GHz and <strong>20 MHz</strong> on 2.4 GHz to reduce interference and retries.</p>

      <h3>8) Router Placement</h3>

      <p>Place high and central, away from microwaves/metal. Avoid inside cabinets. Aim antennas perpendicular (one vertical, one horizontal) for MIMO diversity.</p>

      <h3>9) Separate SSIDs</h3>

      <p>Name 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz differently (e.g., <span class="kbd">Home_24G</span> / <span class="kbd">Home_5G</span>) so phones don’t roam to the slower band.</p>

      <h3>10) Enable QoS / Smart Queue</h3>

      <p>On supported routers, enable <strong>QoS</strong> or <strong>SQM</strong> to prioritize interactive traffic (video calls/gaming) and stop one device from hogging bandwidth.</p>

      <h3>11) Update Router Firmware</h3>

      <p>Manufacturers patch stability and Wi-Fi driver bugs. A firmware refresh often boosts throughput and range.</p>

      <h2>Gaming: Lower Your Ping</h2>

      <ul>

        <li><strong>Prefer 4G/5G over congested public Wi-Fi</strong> for competitive play.</li>

        <li><strong>Close background sync</strong> (Drive/Photos). Enable <span class="kbd">Do Not Disturb</span> to reduce random CPU wakeups.</li>

        <li><strong>Try WARP (1.1.1.1)</strong> for congested local routes. If ping rises, turn it off — choose what your region prefers.</li>

        <li><strong>Use game servers closest to you</strong> (region selection inside the game).</li>

        <li><strong>Lock 60 fps</strong> only if your device can sustain it; otherwise 30–45 fps can be smoother with fewer spikes.</li>

      </ul>

      <h2>Advanced (Power Users — Optional)</h2>

      <h3>12) Band-Locking (LTE)</h3>

      <p>Some devices/apps allow locking to a specific LTE band that’s less congested. Only try if you know your local bands; incorrect locks can drop signal.</p>

      <h3>13) Disable 3G Fallback</h3>

      <p>For modern regions, force <strong>LTE/NR only</strong> in developer/hidden menus to avoid 3G/2G fallback that tanks speeds.</p>

      <h3>14) Clean Network Stats</h3>

      <p>Reset network settings: <span class="kbd">Settings → System → Reset options → Reset Wi-Fi, mobile & Bluetooth</span>. You’ll re-enter Wi-Fi passwords afterward.</p>

      <h3>15) Per-App DNS/VPN</h3>

      <p>Some apps let you choose DNS/VPN per app (e.g., browser-only). This isolates the benefit without slowing other traffic.</p>

      <h2>Streaming & Downloading Optimizations</h2>

      <ul>

        <li><strong>Pick the right CDN</strong>: Some apps let you choose a download server/region. Test nearby regions for better consistency.</li>

        <li><strong>Use parallel downloads</strong> in browsers that support it (e.g., segmented downloads) — but don’t overdo connections on weak Wi-Fi.</li>

        <li><strong>Cap video at 720p</strong> on mobile to avoid buffering when signal fluctuates.</li>

        <li><strong>Cache-friendly habits</strong>: Play the first 5–10 seconds, pause for 20–30 seconds to buffer, then continue.</li>

      </ul>

      <h2>Security vs Speed (Finding the Balance)</h2>

      <p>Ad-blocking, traffic filtering, and VPN encryption can cost 5–25% speed. For maximum speed, disable them temporarily on trusted networks; for risky/public networks, keep them on and accept the small speed trade-off.</p>

      <h2>Myths & Mistakes to Avoid</h2>

      <ul>

        <li><strong>Task killer apps</strong> — frequently killing apps increases CPU wakeups and can worsen performance.</li>

        <li><strong>Signal bars = speed</strong> — full bars on a congested cell can still be slow. Congestion matters more than raw signal.</li>

        <li><strong>VPN always faster</strong> — only true if your ISP route is congested; otherwise it adds overhead.</li>

      </ul>

      <h2>Test Properly: Measure What Improves</h2>

      <ol class="steps">

        <li>Use the <strong>same server</strong> for comparisons (choose a nearby test server and stick with it).</li>

        <li>Run each test <strong>3 times</strong> and average the results.</li>

        <li>Test both <strong>download</strong>, <strong>upload</strong>, and <strong>latency</strong> (ping + jitter).</li>

        <li>Repeat at <strong>peak vs off-peak</strong> hours to understand congestion patterns.</li>

      </ol>

      <h2>Checklist: 21 Fixes at a Glance</h2>

      <ul>

        <li>Airplane mode refresh</li>

        <li>Phone restart</li>

        <li>Move closer to router / switch bands</li>

        <li>Disable unnecessary VPN</li>

        <li>Close heavy background apps</li>

        <li>Lock network mode (5G/4G vs 4G only)</li>

        <li>Clean & correct APN</li>

        <li>Force new cell/band by repositioning</li>

        <li>Use SIM slot 1</li>

        <li>Disable Data Saver temporarily</li>

        <li>Private DNS: <span class="kbd">dns.google</span> / <span class="kbd">one.one.one.one</span></li>

        <li>Browser/app cache clean & Lite modes</li>

        <li>Router: best channel & width</li>

        <li>Router placement & antenna angle</li>

        <li>Separate SSIDs (2.4 vs 5 GHz)</li>

        <li>Enable QoS / SQM</li>

        <li>Update router firmware</li>

        <li>Prefer mobile data for gaming (over public Wi-Fi)</li>

        <li>Try WARP if local routes are bad</li>

        <li>Network reset (last resort)</li>

        <li>Advanced band-lock (optional)</li>

      </ul>

      <div class="warn"><strong>Note:</strong> Don’t change hidden phone menus you don’t understand. Write down default values before edits so you can revert.</div>

      <h2>FAQ</h2>

      <h3>Why is my internet fast on speed tests but slow in apps?</h3>

      <p>CDN or route differences. Fix with better DNS, or try a VPN only for the affected app. Also check app-specific quality settings and background tasks.</p>

      <h3>Is 5 GHz always better than 2.4 GHz?</h3>

      <p>No. 5 GHz is faster but shorter range. If you’re far from the router, 2.4 GHz may be more stable.</p>

      <h3>Will a VPN increase my speed?</h3>

      <p>Only if your ISP path is congested or throttled. Otherwise, VPN adds encryption overhead and may reduce speed slightly.</p>

      <h3>What Private DNS should I use?</h3>

      <p>Start with <span class="kbd">dns.google</span> or <span class="kbd">one.one.one.one</span>. Keep the one that loads your everyday sites fastest.</p>

      <h3>Do “signal booster” apps work?</h3>

      <p>No. They can’t change radio hardware. Use the real fixes above.</p>

    </article>

    <aside class="card col-4">

      <h2>At-a-Glance Setup</h2>

      <ol>

        <li>Set <strong>Private DNS</strong> to <span class="kbd">dns.google</span>.</li>

        <li>Lock <strong>4G/5G (Auto)</strong> or <strong>4G only</strong> if 5G is unstable.</li>

        <li>Clean APN → keep one default.</li>

        <li>On Wi-Fi → use 5 GHz + channel 36/40/44/48.</li>

        <li>Enable router <strong>QoS</strong>.</li>

      </ol>

      <h2>Labels</h2>

      <div class="labels">

        <span class="chip">Tech Tricks</span>

